var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#Alphalytics","page":"Home","text":"Documentation for Alphalytics.\n\n","title":"Alphalytics"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.alpha","page":"Home","text":"alpha(returns::TimeArray, benchmark_returns::TimeArray, risk_free_rate::Number = 0.0) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute each asset's return in excess of what its beta alone would predict.\n\nalpha = mean asset return - [risk-free rate + beta * (mean benchmark return - risk-free rate)]\n\nPositive alpha is the part of performance not explained by simply taking on benchmark exposure, which is the part attributable to skill or to the factor itself.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nbenchmark_returns::TimeArray: Single-column benchmark returns on the same timestamps.\nrisk_free_rate::Number: Per-period risk-free rate (default: 0.0).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One alpha per column, in column order, expressed per period in the same   units as the input returns.\n\nThrows\n\nErrorException: If the timestamps of the two TimeArrays do not match.\nAssertionError: If either TimeArray contains NaN values.\n\nNotes\n\nrisk_free_rate is a per-period rate, matching the frequency of the returns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.alpha"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.annualized_downside_deviation","page":"Home","text":"annualized_downside_deviation(returns::TimeArray, mar::Number = 0, periods_per_year::Int = 52;\n                              corrected::Bool = true) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the annualized downside deviation of each asset.\n\nThe periodic figure from downside_deviation scaled by the square root of periods_per_year.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nmar::Number: Per-period minimum acceptable return (default: 0).\nperiods_per_year::Int: Number of periods in a year (default: 52, i.e. weekly data).\ncorrected::Bool: Apply Bessel's correction (default: true).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One annualized downside deviation per column, in column order.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If returns has fewer than two data points, or periods_per_year is   not positive.\n\nNotes\n\nmar is a per-period threshold. To use an annual target, divide it by periods_per_year   before passing it in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.annualized_downside_deviation"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.annualized_return-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, Int64}","page":"Home","text":"annualized_return(returns::TimeArray, periods_per_year::Int) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the compound annual growth rate of each asset.\n\nThe returns are compounded into a total growth figure, then rescaled to a one-year horizon, giving the annual rate that would have produced the same result over the sample.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nperiods_per_year::Int: Number of periods in a year (252 daily, 52 weekly, 12 monthly).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One annualized return per column, in column order.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If returns contains fewer than two data points.\n\nNotes\n\nThe whole sample length is used for every column, even if some columns start later. Drop   leading NaNs first if you need columns to be compared over identical windows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.annualized_return"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.annualized_sharpe_ratio-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, Int64}","page":"Home","text":"annualized_sharpe_ratio(returns::TimeArray, periods_per_year::Int; risk_free_rate = 0.0) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the annualized Sharpe ratio of each asset.\n\nSharpe = (annualized return - risk-free rate) / annualized standard deviation\n\nThis is the conventionally quoted form, built from annualized_return and annualized_stdev rather than by scaling a per-period ratio.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nperiods_per_year::Int: Number of periods in a year (252 daily, 52 weekly, 12 monthly).\nrisk_free_rate: Annual risk-free rate, subtracted from the annualized return   (default: 0.0).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One annualized Sharpe ratio per column, in column order.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If returns has fewer than two data points, or periods_per_year is   not positive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.annualized_sharpe_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.annualized_stdev-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, Int64}","page":"Home","text":"annualized_stdev(returns::TimeArray, periods_per_year::Int) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the annualized volatility of each asset.\n\nThe periodic standard deviation from stdev is scaled by the square root of periods_per_year, the standard convention for putting volatility on an annual footing.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nperiods_per_year::Int: Number of periods in a year (252 daily, 52 weekly, 12 monthly).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One annualized standard deviation per column, in column order.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If returns contains fewer than two data points.\n\nNotes\n\nThe square-root-of-time rule assumes returns are serially uncorrelated. Trending or   mean-reverting series will have their true annual volatility under- or overstated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.annualized_stdev"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.arithmetic_return-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"arithmetic_return(returns::TimeArray) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the simple average periodic return of each asset.\n\nThis is the plain mean of the return series. It overstates realised growth whenever returns vary, because it ignores compounding; geom_return is the compounded counterpart.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One mean return per column, in column order. NaN returns are ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.arithmetic_return"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.arithmetic_sharpe_ratio","page":"Home","text":"arithmetic_sharpe_ratio(returns::TimeArray, risk_free_rate::Number = 0) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the per-period Sharpe ratio of each asset, using the arithmetic mean return.\n\nSharpe = (mean return - risk-free rate) / standard deviation\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nrisk_free_rate::Number: Per-period risk-free rate (default: 0).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One Sharpe ratio per column, in column order, on a per-period basis.   A column with zero volatility yields NaN.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If returns has fewer than two data points, or holds non-numeric data.\n\nNotes\n\nThe result is per period, not annual. Use annualized_sharpe_ratio to compare   against the figures usually quoted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.arithmetic_sharpe_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.beta-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"beta(returns::TimeArray, benchmark_returns::TimeArray) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute each asset's sensitivity to the benchmark.\n\nbeta = cov(asset returns, benchmark returns) / var(benchmark returns)\n\nA beta of 1 moves with the benchmark, above 1 amplifies it, below 1 damps it, and a negative beta moves against it.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nbenchmark_returns::TimeArray: Single-column benchmark returns on the same timestamps.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One beta per column, in column order. A benchmark with zero variance   yields NaN.\n\nThrows\n\nErrorException: If the timestamps of the two TimeArrays do not match.\nAssertionError: If either TimeArray contains NaN values. Drop or fill them first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.beta"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.column_mean-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"column_mean(ta::TimeArray) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCalculate the mean of each column in a TimeArray, ignoring NaN values.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: Input TimeArray containing data with possible NaN values.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One mean per column, in column order. This is the same shape as the other column statistics such as stdev and downside_deviation. Pair it with colnames(ta) to label the results.\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([1.0 2.0 NaN; 4.0 NaN 6.0], timestamp=1:2, colnames=[:A, :B, :C])\ncolumn_mean(ta)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.column_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.compute_quantiles_returns-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, TimeSeries.TimeArray, Int64}","page":"Home","text":"compute_quantiles_returns(quantiles_ta::TimeArray, returns_ta::TimeArray, n_quantiles::Int) -> TimeArray\n\nBuild the return series of every quantile portfolio at once.\n\nThis applies quantile_return to each quantile from 1 to n_quantiles and merges the results, giving the return panel that the rest of the quantile analysis consumes.\n\nArguments\n\nquantiles_ta::TimeArray: Quantile assignments, one column per security, already lagged   to line up with the returns they earn.\nreturns_ta::TimeArray: Realised returns, same shape and timestamps as quantiles_ta.\nn_quantiles::Int: Number of quantiles to compute.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: One column per quantile, named Q1 through Q<n_quantiles>.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If the two TimeArrays do not have matching timestamps.\n\nExample\n\nquantiles = rowwise_tied_quantiles(factor_scores, 5)\nqrets = compute_quantiles_returns(lag(quantiles, 1), forward_returns, 5)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.compute_quantiles_returns"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.consecutive_values-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, Any, Int64}","page":"Home","text":"consecutive_values(mtx::AbstractMatrix, target::Any, n::Int) -> Vector{Bool}\n\nCheck each column of the matrix mtx for at least n consecutive occurrences of target.\n\nArguments\n\nmtx::AbstractMatrix: The input matrix to search (columns are checked independently).\ntarget::Any: The value to look for consecutive occurrences of.\nn::Int: The number of consecutive occurrences required.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Bool}: A boolean vector where each element indicates if the corresponding column contains at least n consecutive target values.\n\nExample\n\nmtx = [0 1; 1 1; 1 0; 1 1]\nconsecutive_values(mtx, 1, 3) # returns [true, true]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.consecutive_values"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.consecutive_values-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any, Int64}","page":"Home","text":"consecutive_values(ta::AbstractVector, target::Any, n::Int) -> Bool\n\nCheck if there are at least n consecutive occurrences of target in the vector ta.\n\nArguments\n\nta::AbstractVector: The input vector to search.\ntarget::Any: The value to look for consecutive occurrences of.\nn::Int: The number of consecutive occurrences required.\n\nReturns\n\nBool: Returns true if target appears at least n times in a row in ta, otherwise false.\n\nExample\n\nta = [0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 1, 1]\nconsecutive_values(ta, 1, 3) # returns true\nconsecutive_values(ta, 0, 2) # returns false\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.consecutive_values"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.consecutive_values-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, Any, Int64}","page":"Home","text":"consecutive_values(ta::TimeArray, target::Any, n::Int) -> Vector{Bool}\n\nCheck each column of the TimeArray for at least n consecutive occurrences of target.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: The input TimeArray to search (columns are checked independently).\ntarget::Any: The value to look for consecutive occurrences of.\nn::Int: The number of consecutive occurrences required.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Bool}: A boolean vector where each element indicates if the corresponding column contains at least n consecutive target values.\n\nExample\n\nconsecutive_values(returns_ts, 0, 10)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.consecutive_values"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.down_capture","page":"Home","text":"down_capture(returns::TimeArray, benchmark_returns::TimeArray, mar::Number = 0) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute how much of the benchmark's losses each asset absorbed.\n\nOnly the periods where the benchmark fell below mar are considered; the asset's mean return over those periods is divided by the benchmark's. Lower is better: 0.8 means the asset lost only 80% as much as the benchmark did in down markets, while above 1 means it lost more.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nbenchmark_returns::TimeArray: Single-column benchmark returns on the same timestamps.\nmar::Number: Threshold below which the benchmark counts as down (default: 0).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One down capture ratio per column, in column order. All NaN if the   benchmark never fell below mar, and NaN for a benchmark whose mean down return is zero.\n\nThrows\n\nErrorException: If the timestamps of the two TimeArrays do not match.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.down_capture"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.downside_deviation","page":"Home","text":"downside_deviation(returns::TimeArray, mar::Number = 0; corrected::Bool = true) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the volatility of the losses only, ignoring upside moves.\n\nPlain standard deviation punishes large gains as much as large losses. Downside deviation looks solely at returns falling below the minimum acceptable return, which matches how investors actually experience risk. It is the denominator of the Sortino ratio.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nmar::Number: Minimum acceptable return; only returns below this count (default: 0).\ncorrected::Bool: Apply Bessel's correction, dividing by n-1 rather than n (default: true).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One downside deviation per column, in column order. A column with no   returns below mar yields 0.0.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If returns holds non-numeric data.\n\nNotes\n\nThe deviations are divided by the count of below-MAR observations, not by the total   number of periods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.downside_deviation"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.drawdowns-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"drawdowns(returns::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCompute the full drawdown series of each asset: how far below its running peak it sits at every point in time.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: Same timestamps and column names, holding the drawdown at each point as a   negative decimal, where -0.2 means 20% below the peak. A value of 0.0 means the asset is   at a new high.\n\nExample\n\ndd = drawdowns(returns)\nminimum(values(dd))  # the worst point of the deepest drawdown\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.drawdowns"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.geom_return-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"geom_return(returns::TimeArray) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the compounded average periodic return of each asset.\n\nThis is the constant per-period return that would have produced the same total growth as the actual series. Unlike arithmetic_return it accounts for compounding, so it is the honest figure to quote for realised performance.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One geometric mean return per column, in column order. NaN returns are   ignored, and a column with fewer than 2 valid observations yields NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.geom_return"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.geom_sharpe_ratio","page":"Home","text":"geom_sharpe_ratio(returns::TimeArray, risk_free_rate::Number = 0) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the per-period Sharpe ratio of each asset, using the compounded mean return.\n\nSharpe = (geometric mean return - risk-free rate) / standard deviation\n\nIdentical to arithmetic_sharpe_ratio except that the numerator accounts for compounding, which gives a slightly lower and more realistic figure for volatile series.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nrisk_free_rate::Number: Per-period risk-free rate (default: 0).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One Sharpe ratio per column, in column order, on a per-period basis.   A column with zero volatility yields NaN.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If returns has fewer than two data points, or holds non-numeric data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.geom_sharpe_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.max_drawdown-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"max_drawdown(returns::TimeArray) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the deepest peak-to-trough loss each asset suffered over the sample.\n\nThis is the single worst point of drawdowns, and the usual measure of how much pain holding the asset would have involved.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One maximum drawdown per column, in column order, as a positive   decimal: 0.35 means the asset fell 35% below its peak at worst. NaN drawdowns are ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.max_drawdown"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.mean_autocor-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, Int64}","page":"Home","text":"mean_autocor(factors::TimeArray, maxlag::Int) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the average autocorrelation of factor scores across securities.\n\nEach security's own score history is autocorrelated, then the results are averaged across securities lag by lag. High autocorrelation means scores are persistent, which implies low turnover; low autocorrelation means the factor reshuffles its rankings quickly.\n\nArguments\n\nfactors::TimeArray: Factor scores, one column per security.\nmaxlag::Int: Maximum lag for which to compute the autocorrelation.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: Mean autocorrelation per lag, of length maxlag + 1. The first entry   is lag 0 and is therefore always 1.0; lag k sits at index k + 1.\nIf no security has more than maxlag non-NaN scores, a warning is issued and a vector of   maxlag NaNs is returned.\n\nDetails\n\nNaN scores are dropped per security before the autocorrelation is computed, and securities   with maxlag or fewer remaining observations are excluded entirely.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.mean_autocor"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.overall_capture","page":"Home","text":"overall_capture(returns::TimeArray, benchmark_returns::TimeArray, mar::Number = 0) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the capture ratio: upside captured relative to downside suffered.\n\noverall capture = up capture / down capture\n\nThis condenses up_capture and down_capture into one number. Above 1 is favourable, meaning the asset keeps more of the benchmark's gains than of its losses.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nbenchmark_returns::TimeArray: Single-column benchmark returns on the same timestamps.\nmar::Number: Threshold separating up from down markets (default: 0).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One overall capture ratio per column, in column order.\n\nNotes\n\nThe ratio is hard to read when down capture is negative, which happens when an asset gains   on average while the benchmark falls. Inspect the two legs separately in that case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.overall_capture"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.performance_table","page":"Home","text":"performance_table(ta_returns::TimeArray, benchmark_returns::TimeArray, periods_per_year::Int,\n                  mar::Number = 0) -> DataFrame\n\nAssemble the full set of summary performance statistics for a group of assets.\n\nThis is the headline report: return, risk, benchmark-relative and drawdown-based measures collected into one table, one column per asset. See quantile_performance_table for the equivalent report across factor quantiles.\n\nArguments\n\nta_returns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nbenchmark_returns::TimeArray: Single-column benchmark returns on the same timestamps.\nperiods_per_year::Int: Number of periods in a year (252 daily, 52 weekly, 12 monthly).\nmar::Number: Threshold separating up from down markets, also used as the minimum   acceptable return of the Sortino ratio (default: 0).\n\nReturns\n\nDataFrame with a leading Stat column and one column per asset. The rows are Annual   Return, Annual StDev, Sharpe Ratio, Sortino Ratio, Max Drawdowns, Beta, Alpha, Treynor   Ratio, Ulcer Ratio, Tail Ratio, Down Capture, Up Capture and Overall Capture.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If the asset and benchmark row counts do not match.\nAssertionError: If either TimeArray contains NaN values, which beta and alpha reject.\n\nNotes\n\nEvery value is left on its natural scale: returns and drawdowns are decimals, not percent.\nPass the result to plot_performance_table to chart the headline rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.performance_table"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.plot_factor_distribution-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"plot_factor_distribution(factors::TimeArray; bins::Int = 20) -> Plot\n\nPlot the mean factor score over time alongside its distribution.\n\nThe two views answer different questions: the time series shows whether the factor drifts or spikes, while the histogram shows whether its scores are well spread or bunched up.\n\nArguments\n\nfactors::TimeArray: Factor scores, one column per security.\nbins::Int: Number of bins for the histogram (default: 20).\n\nReturns\n\nA 2x1 plot: the cross-sectional mean score per timestamp above, its histogram below.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: If factors is empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.plot_factor_distribution"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.plot_performance_table-Tuple{DataFrames.DataFrame}","page":"Home","text":"plot_performance_table(table::DataFrame) -> Plot\n\nPlot a bar chart per headline statistic of a summary performance table.\n\nArguments\n\ntable::DataFrame: A table as returned by quantile_performance_table or   performance_table: a leading Stat column naming each row, then one column   per quantile or asset.\n\nReturns\n\nA 3x2 grid of bar charts, one per plotted statistic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.plot_performance_table"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.quantile_chg-Tuple{DataFrames.DataFrame}","page":"Home","text":"quantile_chg(perf_table::DataFrame) -> DataFrame\n\nMeasure how steadily each statistic changes as you step from one quantile to the next.\n\nA factor that works should improve monotonically across quantiles, so the step-to-step change in each statistic is taken, then summarised by its mean, its standard deviation, and their ratio. A large positive ratio means the statistic climbs consistently rather than erratically.\n\nArguments\n\nperf_table::DataFrame: A table as returned by quantile_performance_table:   a leading Stat column naming each row, then one column per quantile in order.\n\nReturns\n\nDataFrame with one row per statistic and the columns Stat, MeanChg, StdChg   and Ratio (MeanChg / StdChg).\n\nwarning: Not currently runnable\nThis function calls shift_dataframe, which is not defined in Alphalytics or in any of its dependencies, and refers to an undefined q_perf_table on its last few lines. Both need resolving before it can be called.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.quantile_chg"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.quantile_performance_table-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"quantile_performance_table(quantile_returns::TimeArray, benchmark_returns::TimeArray;\n                           thresh_value::Number = 0, periods_per_year::Int) -> DataFrame\n\nCompute table with summary performance statistics for factor quantiles.\n\nArguments\n\nquantile_returns::TimeArray: Returns for each quantile, one column per quantile.\nbenchmark_returns::TimeArray: Benchmark returns, aligned to quantile_returns.\nthresh_value::Number: Threshold separating up from down markets, also used as the   minimum acceptable return of the Sortino ratio (default: 0).\nperiods_per_year::Int: Number of return periods in a year (e.g. 52 for weekly data).\n\nReturns\n\nDataFrame with one row per statistic and one column per quantile, plus a leading   Stat column naming each row.\n\nDetails\n\nThe return, standard deviation and drawdown rows are reported in percent. The Sharpe,   Sortino and capture rows are ratios and are left on their natural scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.quantile_performance_table"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.quantile_return-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, TimeSeries.TimeArray, Any}","page":"Home","text":"quantile_return(quantiles_ts::TimeArray, returns_ts::TimeArray, quantile::Any) -> TimeArray\n\nCalculate the equally weighted mean return of the securities sitting in one quantile.\n\nFor each timestamp the securities assigned to quantile are selected and their returns averaged, giving the return series of a portfolio that holds that quantile.\n\nArguments\n\nquantiles_ts::TimeArray: Quantile assignments, one column per security. These must   already be lagged so that a period's assignment lines up with the return it earns;   pairing an assignment with the return of the same period looks ahead.\nreturns_ts::TimeArray: Realised returns, same shape and timestamps as quantiles_ts.\nquantile::Any: The quantile to select, matched against the values of quantiles_ts.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A single column named Q<quantile> holding the mean return per timestamp.   A period with no securities in quantile is NaN.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If the two TimeArrays do not have matching timestamps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.quantile_return"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.quantile_turnover-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, Number}","page":"Home","text":"quantile_turnover(quantile_ta::TimeArray, target_quantile::Number) -> TimeArray\n\nCalculate how much of a single quantile's membership is replaced each period.\n\nA security counts as unchanged when it sat in target_quantile last period and still does this period. Turnover is the fraction of the current members that are new arrivals, so 0.0 means the bucket is unchanged and 1.0 means it turned over completely.\n\nArguments\n\nquantile_ta::TimeArray: Quantile assignments, one column per security, ordered by time.\ntarget_quantile::Number: The quantile whose turnover is measured.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray with three columns, starting one period after the input because the first   period has nothing to compare against:\nTurnover: Fraction of the quantile's members that changed from the previous period.\nNoChange: Count of members carried over from the previous period.\nTotal: Count of securities in the quantile this period.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: If quantile_ta has fewer than 2 time points.\n\nNotes\n\nA period in which no security sits in target_quantile divides by zero and yields NaN   for that period's turnover.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.quantile_turnover"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.quantiles_turnover-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"quantiles_turnover(quantile_ta::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCalculate the turnover of every quantile, side by side.\n\nThis runs quantile_turnover for each quantile from 1 up to the largest one present in the data and keeps only the Turnover column of each, so the quantiles can be compared directly. Extreme quantiles usually turn over faster than middle ones.\n\nArguments\n\nquantile_ta::TimeArray: Quantile assignments, one column per security, ordered by time.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: One column per quantile, named Q1 through Q<max>, holding that quantile's   turnover fraction. Starts one period after the input.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: If quantile_ta has fewer than 2 time points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.quantiles_turnover"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.returns_to_prices","page":"Home","text":"returns_to_prices(returns::TimeArray, init_value::Int = 1) -> TimeArray\n\nCompute the cumulative product of returns, preserving NaN locations.\n\nThis function treats the input as returns (as decimals or percentages), so it adds 1 to each value before multiplying.   NaN values are preserved in the output, and the cumulative product continues from the last non-NaN value after a NaN.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: TimeArray containing return data with possible NaN values.\ninit_value::Int: Initial value for the cumulative product (default: 1).\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: TimeArray with cumulative products, preserving NaN locations.\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([0.1, NaN, 0.05, -0.02], timestamp=1:4, colnames=[:R])\nreturns_to_prices(ta)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.returns_to_prices"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.rolling_mean_autocor-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, Int64, Int64}","page":"Home","text":"rolling_mean_autocor(factors::TimeArray, acf_maxlag::Int, window::Int) -> TimeArray\n\nTrack how factor persistence changes over time by computing mean_autocor on a rolling window.\n\nArguments\n\nfactors::TimeArray: Factor scores, one column per security.\nacf_maxlag::Int: The maximum lag for which to compute autocorrelations.\nwindow::Int: Number of observations in each rolling window.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: One column per lag, named ACF1 through ACF<acf_maxlag>, and one row per   window. Lag 0 is dropped, since it is always 1.0. Each row is stamped with the timestamp   of the last observation in its window, so the series starts at timestamp(factors)[window].\n\nThrows\n\nErrorException: If factors has fewer than window + acf_maxlag observations.\n\nDetails\n\nA window whose autocorrelation cannot be computed is warned about and left uninitialised   rather than aborting the whole series.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rolling_mean_autocor"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.row_mean-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"row_mean(ta::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCalculate the mean of each row in a TimeArray, ignoring NaN values.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: Input TimeArray containing data with possible NaN values.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A new TimeArray with the same timestamps as the input, where each value represents  the mean of non-NaN values in the corresponding row of the input.\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([1.0 2.0 NaN; 4.0 NaN 6.0], timestamp=1:2, colnames=[:A, :B, :C])\nrow_mean(ta)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.row_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.rows_pearsonr-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"rows_pearsonr(factors::TimeArray, returns::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCompute the cross-sectional (row-wise) Pearson correlation between factors and returns.\n\nThis is the linear counterpart of rows_spearmanr. It measures the strength of the linear relationship between factor score and realised return, so unlike the rank IC it is sensitive to outliers and to the scale of the factor.\n\nArguments\n\nfactors::TimeArray: Factor scores, one column per security.\nreturns::TimeArray: Realised returns, same dimensions and timestamps as factors.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A single column named :Pearson_IC holding one correlation per timestamp.   A row is NaN when fewer than two securities have both a factor score and a return.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If factors and returns do not have the same dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rows_pearsonr"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.rows_spearmanr-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"rows_spearmanr(factors::TimeArray, returns::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCompute the cross-sectional (row-wise) Spearman rank correlation between factors and returns.\n\nThis is the rank information coefficient (IC): for each timestamp, the securities are ranked by factor score and by realised return, and the two rankings are correlated. Being rank based, it measures monotonic association and is insensitive to outliers.\n\nArguments\n\nfactors::TimeArray: Factor scores, one column per security.\nreturns::TimeArray: Realised returns, same dimensions and timestamps as factors.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A single column named :SpearmanRank_IC holding one correlation per timestamp.   A row is NaN when fewer than two securities have both a factor score and a return.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If factors and returns do not have the same dimensions.\n\nExample\n\nic = rows_spearmanr(factor_scores, forward_returns)\nmean(filter(!isnan, values(ic)))  # average IC over the sample\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rows_spearmanr"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.rowwise_competerank-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"rowwise_competerank(ta::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCompute \"competition\" ranks (also known as standard competition ranks or \"1224\" ranking) for each value in the TimeArray along rows, ignoring NaNs.\n\nFor each row, non-NaN values are ranked in ascending order (smallest gets rank 1, ties get the same rank, and the next rank is incremented by the number of tied values). NaN positions remain NaN in the output.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: A TimeArray containing numerical data.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A new TimeArray with the same timestamps and column names as the input, but with values replaced by their row-wise competition ranks. NaN values are preserved in their original positions.\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([1.0 3.0 NaN; 2.0 1.0 4.0], timestamp=1:2, colnames=[:A, :B, :C])\nrowwise_competerank(ta)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rowwise_competerank"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.rowwise_count-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, Any}","page":"Home","text":"rowwise_count(ta::TimeArray, target_value::Any) -> TimeArray\n\nCount the occurrences of target_value in each row of a TimeArray.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: The TimeArray to analyze.\ntarget_value::Any: The value to count within each row. If target_value === NaN, counts NaN values.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A new TimeArray where each entry represents the count of target_value (or NaN) in  the corresponding row of the input TimeArray. The timestamp from the input TimeArray is preserved.\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([1 2 NaN; NaN 2 1; 1 1 1], timestamp=1:3, colnames=[:A, :B, :C])\nrowwise_count(ta, 1)      # Counts 1s in each row\nrowwise_count(ta, NaN)    # Counts NaNs in each row\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rowwise_count"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.rowwise_countall-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"rowwise_countall(ta::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCount the occurrences of all unique (non-NaN) values in each row of a TimeArray.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: The input TimeArray to analyze.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A new TimeArray where each column represents the count of a unique value (excluding NaN) in each row of the input. The timestamp is preserved.\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([1 2 1; 2 2 1; 1 1 1], timestamp=1:3, colnames=[:A, :B, :C])\nrowwise_countall(ta)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rowwise_countall"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.rowwise_denserank-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"rowwise_denserank(ta::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCompute dense ranks for each value in the TimeArray along rows, ignoring NaNs.\n\nFor each row, non-NaN values are ranked in ascending order using dense ranking (no gaps in ranks for ties). NaN positions remain NaN in the output.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: A TimeArray containing numerical data.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A new TimeArray with the same timestamps and column names as the input, but with values replaced by their row-wise dense ranks. NaN values are preserved in their original positions.\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([1.0 3.0 NaN; 2.0 1.0 4.0], timestamp=1:2, colnames=[:A, :B, :C])\nrowwise_denserank(ta)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rowwise_denserank"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.rowwise_ordinal_pctrank-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"rowwise_ordinal_pctrank(ta::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCompute ordinal percentile ranks for each value in the TimeArray along rows, ignoring NaNs.\n\nFor each row, non-NaN values are ranked ordinally, and their percentile rank is calculated as (ordinal rank) / (maximum rank in the row). NaN values are preserved in their original positions.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: TimeArray containing numerical data.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A new TimeArray with ordinal percentile ranks for each row.\n\nNotes\n\nRows with only one non-NaN value will result in NaN for that row's percentile calculation.\nNaN values are preserved in their original positions.\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([1.0 3.0 NaN; 2.0 1.0 4.0], timestamp=1:2, colnames=[:A, :B, :C])\nrowwise_ordinal_pctrank(ta)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rowwise_ordinal_pctrank"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.rowwise_ordinal_quantiles","page":"Home","text":"rowwise_ordinal_quantiles(ta::TimeArray, n_quantiles::Int=5) -> TimeArray\n\nAssign each value in a TimeArray to a quantile (1 to n_quantiles) within its row, ignoring NaNs.\n\nFor each row, non-NaN values are assigned to quantiles based on their ordinal percentile rank. NaN values remain NaN.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: Input TimeArray.\nn_quantiles::Int: Number of quantiles (default 5).\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: Same shape as input, with quantile numbers (1 = lowest, n_quantiles = highest).\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([1.0 3.0 NaN; 2.0 1.0 4.0], timestamp=1:2, colnames=[:A, :B, :C])\nrowwise_ordinal_quantiles(ta, 3)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rowwise_ordinal_quantiles"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.rowwise_ordinalrank-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"rowwise_ordinalrank(ta::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCompute ordinal ranks for each value in the TimeArray along rows, ignoring NaNs.\n\nFor each row, non-NaN values are ranked in ascending order (smallest gets rank 1). NaN positions remain NaN in the output.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: A TimeArray containing numerical data.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A new TimeArray with the same timestamps and column names as the input, but with values replaced by their row-wise ordinal ranks. NaN values are preserved in their original positions.\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([1.0 3.0 NaN; 2.0 1.0 4.0], timestamp=1:2, colnames=[:A, :B, :C])\nrowwise_ordinalrank(ta)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rowwise_ordinalrank"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.rowwise_tied_pctrank-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"rowwise_tied_pctrank(ta::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCompute tied percentile ranks for each value in the TimeArray along rows, ignoring NaNs.\n\nFor each row, non-NaN values are ranked using tied ranking, and their percentile rank is calculated as (tied rank) / (maximum rank in the row). NaN values are preserved in their original positions.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: TimeArray containing numerical data.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A new TimeArray with tied percentile ranks for each row.\n\nNotes\n\nRows with only one non-NaN value will result in NaN for that row's percentile calculation.\nNaN values are preserved in their original positions.\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([1.0 3.0 NaN; 2.0 1.0 4.0], timestamp=1:2, colnames=[:A, :B, :C])\nrowwise_tied_pctrank(ta)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rowwise_tied_pctrank"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.rowwise_tied_quantiles","page":"Home","text":"rowwise_tied_quantiles(ta::TimeArray, n_quantiles::Int=5) -> TimeArray\n\nAssign each value in a TimeArray to a quantile (1 to n_quantiles) within its row, using tied percentile ranks, ignoring NaNs.\n\nFor each row, non-NaN values are assigned to quantiles based on their tied percentile rank. NaN values remain NaN.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: Input TimeArray.\nn_quantiles::Int: Number of quantiles (default 5).\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: Same shape as input, with quantile numbers (1 = lowest, n_quantiles = highest).\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([1.0 3.0 NaN; 2.0 1.0 4.0], timestamp=1:2, colnames=[:A, :B, :C])\nrowwise_tiedquantiles(ta, 3)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rowwise_tied_quantiles"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.rowwise_tiedrank-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"rowwise_tiedrank(ta::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCompute tied ranks for each value in the TimeArray along rows, ignoring NaNs.\n\nFor each row, non-NaN values are ranked in ascending order, with ties receiving the average of the ranks they span (tied ranking). NaN positions remain NaN in the output.\n\nArguments\n\nta::TimeArray: A TimeArray containing numerical data.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A new TimeArray with the same timestamps and column names as the input, but with values replaced by their row-wise tied ranks. NaN values are preserved in their original positions.\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([1.0 3.0 NaN; 2.0 1.0 4.0], timestamp=1:2, colnames=[:A, :B, :C])\nrowwise_tiedrank(ta)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.rowwise_tiedrank"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.sortino_ratio-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"sortino_ratio(returns::TimeArray; risk_free_rate::Number = 0.0, mar::Number = 0.0,\n              corrected::Bool = true) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the per-period Sortino ratio of each asset.\n\nSortino = (mean return - risk-free rate) / downside deviation\n\nThe Sortino ratio is the Sharpe ratio with downside_deviation in the denominator instead of total volatility, so an asset is not penalised for large gains.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nrisk_free_rate::Number: Per-period risk-free rate (default: 0.0).\nmar::Number: Per-period minimum acceptable return defining the downside (default: 0.0).\ncorrected::Bool: Apply Bessel's correction in the downside deviation (default: true).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One Sortino ratio per column, in column order, on a per-period basis.   A column with no downside (zero downside deviation) yields NaN.\n\nNotes\n\nAll arguments are keywords. Call it as sortino_ratio(returns, mar = 0.01), not   positionally.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.sortino_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.spearman_factor_decay-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, TimeSeries.TimeArray, Int64}","page":"Home","text":"spearman_factor_decay(factors::TimeArray, returns::TimeArray, max_lags::Int) -> Vector{Pair{Int, Float64}}\n\nMeasure how quickly a factor's predictive power decays as it ages.\n\nFor each lag from 1 to max_lags the factor is shifted forward in time, re-aligned with the returns it would then be predicting, and the mean cross-sectional rank IC is taken. A series that holds its IC over many lags is slow to decay and tolerates less frequent rebalancing.\n\nArguments\n\nfactors::TimeArray: Factor scores, one column per security.\nreturns::TimeArray: Realised returns, same dimensions and timestamps as factors.\nmax_lags::Int: Largest lag to evaluate.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Pair{Int, Float64}}: One lag => mean IC pair per lag, ordered from lag 1 to   max_lags.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If factors and returns do not have the same dimensions.\n\nExample\n\ndecay = spearman_factor_decay(factor_scores, forward_returns, 12)\nlast.(decay)  # the mean IC at each lag, as a plain vector\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.spearman_factor_decay"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.stdev-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"stdev(returns::TimeArray) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the periodic volatility of each asset.\n\nThis is the sample standard deviation of the return series, in the same units as the returns themselves. Use annualized_stdev to put it on an annual footing.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One standard deviation per column, in column order. NaN returns are   ignored, and a column with fewer than 2 valid observations yields NaN.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If returns has fewer than two data points, or holds non-numeric data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.stdev"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.tail_ratio","page":"Home","text":"tail_ratio(returns::TimeArray, benchmark_returns::TimeArray, percentile::Float64 = 0.95) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompare how each asset behaves in the benchmark's best periods versus its worst.\n\ntail ratio = mean return in the benchmark's best tail / |mean return in its worst tail|\n\nThe tails are defined by the benchmark, not by the asset's own returns, so this measures behaviour in extreme markets rather than the shape of the asset's own distribution. Above 1 means the asset gains more in the best periods than it loses in the worst.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nbenchmark_returns::TimeArray: Single-column benchmark returns on the same timestamps.\npercentile::Float64: Upper tail cutoff (default: 0.95). The lower tail is its complement,   so the default compares the benchmark's best 5% of periods against its worst 5%.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One tail ratio per column, in column order. All NaN if either tail is   empty, and NaN for a column whose mean worst-tail return is zero.\n\nThrows\n\nErrorException: If the timestamps of the two TimeArrays do not match.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.tail_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.to_cummax-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"to_cummax(returns::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCompute the running peak of the growth curve implied by a series of returns.\n\nNote that the input is returns, not levels: the returns are compounded with to_cumulative_growth first, then the running maximum is taken. This high-water mark is what drawdowns are measured against.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset, possibly with NaN.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: Same timestamps and column names, holding the highest growth value seen up to   and including each timestamp.\n\nDetails\n\nNaN inputs stay NaN in the output, and each column starts its running maximum at its own   first non-NaN entry, so columns with different start dates are handled independently.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.to_cummax"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.to_cumulative_growth","page":"Home","text":"to_cumulative_growth(returns::TimeArray, init_value::Number = 1) -> TimeArray\n\nCompound a series of returns into a growth curve, preserving NaN locations.\n\nEach value is treated as a return, so 1 is added before multiplying. The result is the value an initial investment of init_value would have grown to by each timestamp.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset, possibly with NaN.\ninit_value::Number: Starting value of the growth curve (default: 1).\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: Same timestamps and column names, holding the compounded value at each point.\n\nDetails\n\nNaN inputs stay NaN in the output, and compounding resumes from the last non-NaN value   rather than restarting, so a gap in the data does not reset the curve.\n\nExample\n\nta = TimeArray([0.1, NaN, 0.05], timestamp = 1:3, colnames = [:R])\nto_cumulative_growth(ta)  # 1.1, NaN, 1.155\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.to_cumulative_growth"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.to_pctchange","page":"Home","text":"to_pctchange(prices::TimeArray, window::Int64 = 1) -> TimeArray\n\nCalculate the percentage change of prices over a specified window.\n\nArguments\n\nprices::TimeArray: A TimeArray containing price data. Can be single or multi-column.\nwindow::Int64: The size of the window over which to calculate the percentage change.   Must be at least 1.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray: A new TimeArray with the same timestamps and column names as the input,   but with values representing the percentage changes.\n\nDetails\n\nMissing values in the input are replaced with NaN.\nPercentage change is calculated as (currentprice / laggedprice) - 1.\nThe first window rows of the result are NaN, since there is not enough history to   compute a change for them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.to_pctchange"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.total_quantiles_turnover-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"total_quantiles_turnover(quantile_ta::TimeArray) -> TimeArray\n\nCalculate turnover across the whole universe rather than within one quantile.\n\nA security counts as unchanged when it stays in the same quantile from one period to the next, whichever quantile that is. This is the single headline number for how much the factor reshuffles its rankings, and so for the trading it implies.\n\nArguments\n\nquantile_ta::TimeArray: Quantile assignments, one column per security, ordered by time.\n\nReturns\n\nTimeArray with three columns, starting one period after the input:\nTurnover: Fraction of securities that changed quantile since the previous period.\nNoChange: Count of securities that stayed in the same quantile.\nTotal: Count of securities with a valid (non-NaN) assignment this period.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError: If quantile_ta has fewer than 2 time points.\n\nNotes\n\nTurnover is 1 minus the ratio of unchanged securities to the total with valid entries,   so a period in which every security is NaN yields NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.total_quantiles_turnover"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.treynor_ratio","page":"Home","text":"treynor_ratio(returns::TimeArray, benchmark_returns::TimeArray, periods_per_year::Int,\n              risk_free_rate::Number = 0) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute each asset's excess return per unit of systematic risk.\n\nTreynor = (annualized return - risk-free rate) / beta\n\nWhere the Sharpe ratio divides by total volatility, the Treynor ratio divides by beta alone. It therefore rewards only the market risk taken, which suits assets held inside an already diversified portfolio.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nbenchmark_returns::TimeArray: Single-column benchmark returns on the same timestamps.\nperiods_per_year::Int: Number of periods in a year (252 daily, 52 weekly, 12 monthly).\nrisk_free_rate::Number: Annual risk-free rate (default: 0).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One Treynor ratio per column, in column order. A column with zero beta   yields NaN.\n\nThrows\n\nErrorException: If the timestamps of the two TimeArrays do not match.\nAssertionError: If either TimeArray contains NaN values.\n\nNotes\n\nThe ratio is meaningless for a near-zero or negative beta, where small changes in beta swing   the result wildly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.treynor_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.ulcer_index-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray}","page":"Home","text":"ulcer_index(returns::TimeArray) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nMeasure the depth and the duration of each asset's drawdowns.\n\nulcer index = sqrt(mean(drawdown^2))\n\nmax_drawdown reports only the single worst moment. The ulcer index averages the squared drawdown across every period, so an asset that spends a long time underwater scores worse than one that recovers quickly from an equally deep fall. Lower is better.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One ulcer index per column, in column order, as a positive decimal in   the same units as the drawdowns.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If returns contains fewer than two data points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.ulcer_index"},{"category":"method","location":"#Alphalytics.ulcer_performance_index-Tuple{TimeSeries.TimeArray, Any}","page":"Home","text":"ulcer_performance_index(returns::TimeArray, periods_per_year) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute return per unit of drawdown pain, also known as the Martin ratio.\n\nUPI = annualized return / ulcer index\n\nA Sharpe-like ratio that swaps volatility for ulcer_index, so it judges an asset by how deep and how prolonged its drawdowns were rather than by how much it wobbled.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nperiods_per_year: Number of periods in a year (252 daily, 52 weekly, 12 monthly).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One ulcer performance index per column, in column order. A column that   never drew down (zero ulcer index) yields NaN.\n\nThrows\n\nAssertionError: If returns contains fewer than two data points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.ulcer_performance_index"},{"category":"function","location":"#Alphalytics.up_capture","page":"Home","text":"up_capture(returns::TimeArray, benchmark_returns::TimeArray, mar::Number = 0) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute how much of the benchmark's gains each asset captured.\n\nOnly the periods where the benchmark rose above mar are considered; the asset's mean return over those periods is divided by the benchmark's. Higher is better: 1.2 means the asset gained 20% more than the benchmark did in up markets.\n\nArguments\n\nreturns::TimeArray: Periodic returns as decimals, one column per asset.\nbenchmark_returns::TimeArray: Single-column benchmark returns on the same timestamps.\nmar::Number: Threshold above which the benchmark counts as up (default: 0).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: One up capture ratio per column, in column order. All NaN if the   benchmark never rose above mar, and NaN for a benchmark whose mean up return is zero.\n\nThrows\n\nErrorException: If the timestamps of the two TimeArrays do not match.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Alphalytics.up_capture"}]
}
